.TH POWER4CTL 1 "July 29, 2026" "power4ctl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
power4ctl \- serial and authenticated TCP management tool for power4
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B power4ctl
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR environment-name ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR password-file ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR baud ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.PP
.B power4ctl
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR environment-name ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR password-file ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR baud ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.PP
.B power4ctl
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR environment-name ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR password-file ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR baud ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.B \-D
.RB [ \-i
.IR interval ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR lock\-seconds ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR outdir ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B power4ctl
communicates with a power4 ESP32 device over its serial console or its
authenticated TCP console.
It opens the selected transport, elicits the
.B power4>
prompt, issues a tool-framed command, and returns the result.
.PP
On both serial and TCP transports,
.B power4ctl
sends
.BR "p4exec " command .
The device streams the command output using SMTP-style dot stuffing:
a line beginning with a dot has that dot doubled, and a single dot on a line
by itself terminates the response.
.B power4ctl
removes the stuffing before writing the result to standard output.
The interactive prompt is not used as the response terminator.
Direct human console commands retain the ordinary prompt behavior.
.PP
TCP mode connects to IPv4 port 4244.
The device supplies a random challenge and
.B power4ctl
answers with HMAC-SHA256 using the configured password.
The authenticated session then carries a clear-text console stream.
.PP
For the
.BR "json " commands,
the device responds with a framed-JSON line in the form
.PP
.RS
.EX
P4J1 \fIsize\fP \fIsha1hex\fP \fIjson\fP
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B power4ctl
validates the length and SHA-1 checksum before printing the JSON object
to standard output.
Log lines and other console noise from the device are silently ignored.
.PP
Any command not listed below is sent as a tool-framed console command and all
unstuffed output lines are echoed to standard output through the terminating
dot line.
.PP
When invoked without a command argument,
.B power4ctl
enters an interactive REPL.
Commands are read from standard input using
.BR libedit (3)
line editing with Emacs key bindings and persistent command history
(see
.B FILES
below).
The serial port is opened for each command and closed again before the
next prompt is issued, so other tools may use the port between commands.
Type
.B exit
or
.BR quit ,
or press
.BR Ctrl\-D ,
to leave the REPL.
The same commands accepted on the command line are accepted in the REPL.
.PP
In daemon mode
.RB ( \-D ),
.B power4ctl
runs indefinitely, opening the serial port at each interval, collecting
the JSON reports
.RB ( batteries ,
.BR banks ,
.BR relays ,
.BR inputs ,
.BR parameters ,
.BR logs ),
writing them atomically to the output directory, then closing the port
and sleeping until the next interval.
Each file is written to a hidden temporary name and then renamed into
place so readers never see a partial file.
If the port cannot be locked within the lock-wait timeout the cycle is
skipped and the existing files are left unchanged.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Serial port to use.
Default:
.IR /dev/ttyACM0 .
.TP
.BI \-a " address"
Use the authenticated TCP console at this IPv4 address instead of serial.
Exactly one of
.B \-e
or
.B \-f
is required.
.TP
.BI \-e " environment-name"
Read the TCP password from the named environment variable.
The value is used exactly and must contain 16 to 128 printable bytes.
.TP
.BI \-f " password-file"
Read the TCP password from a file.
Leading and trailing whitespace is removed before validating the 16 to 128
printable byte password.
.TP
.BI \-b " baud"
Baud rate.
Default:
.IR 115200 .
.TP
.BI \-t " seconds"
Per-operation timeout: how long to wait for the prompt and for each
command response.
Default:
.IR 2 .
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose mode.
Logs every byte sent
.RB ( >>> )
and received
.RB ( <<< )
to standard error, with non-printable characters shown as
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
or
.BR \exNN .
.TP
.B \-D
Daemon mode.
Run forever, collecting all six JSON reports on a periodic loop.
Takes no command arguments.
Terminated cleanly by
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGINT .
.TP
.BI \-i " seconds"
Daemon poll interval in seconds.
Default:
.IR 60 .
.TP
.BI \-l " seconds"
How long to wait for the serial port lock before skipping a daemon cycle.
Default:
.IR 5 .
.TP
.BI \-o " dir"
Directory where daemon mode writes
.IR batteries.json ,
.IR banks.json ,
.IR relays.json ,
.IR inputs.json ,
.IR parameters.json ,
and
.I logs.json
(created if it does not exist).
Default:
.IR /run/power4 .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B json batteries
Request the current battery observations from the device and print the
JSON object to standard output.
.TP
.B json banks
Request the current battery bank state from the device and print the
JSON object to standard output.
.TP
.B json inputs
Request the current digital input state from the device and print the
JSON object to standard output.
.TP
.B json logs
Request a snapshot of the device's recent system log text (a rolling 16 KB
buffer) and print the JSON object to standard output.
The log text is in the object's
.B text
field.
.TP
.B json parameters
Request the currently defined policy parameters and their lifetime state
from the device and print the JSON object to standard output.
.TP
.B json relays
Request the current relay state from the device and print the JSON object
to standard output.
.TP
.BI "stage " filename
Read
.I filename
from disk, compute its SHA-1, and upload it to the device as a staged
policy program.
The file is sent base64-encoded in 76-character lines preceded by a
.B "policy upload \fIsha1hex\fP"
command.
A policy program may contain at most 16 KiB.
The file buffer is allocated on the host heap.
A blank line signals end of upload.
On success, the device's confirmation message is printed to standard output.
.TP
.I anything-else
The command is sent verbatim to the device and all response lines are
echoed to standard output until the
.B power4>
prompt returns.
This provides full access to the device's console without a separate
terminal emulator.
ESP-NOW configuration commands such as
.B "show espnow"
and
.B "set espnow ..."
are available through this passthrough mode; they do not add a separate
.B power4ctl
transport or daemon report.
Use
.B "set espnow rate auto|lr-500|lr-250"
to select the device's persistent outbound ESP-NOW rate.  New firmware always
enables reception of both ordinary and Espressif long-range frames, so the two
directions may use different LR rates.
.SH DAEMON OUTPUT FILES
In daemon mode, six files are maintained under the output directory
(default
.IR /run/power4 ):
.TP
.I batteries.json
Current battery observations.
.TP
.I banks.json
Current battery bank state.
.TP
.I relays.json
Current relay state.
.TP
.I inputs.json
Current digital input state.
.TP
.I parameters.json
Currently defined policy parameters, including value types and lifetimes.
.TP
.I logs.json
Snapshot of the device's recent system log text (rolling 16 KB buffer).
.PP
Each file is written atomically: new content is first written to
.RI .tmp. name .json
in the same directory, then renamed into place.
If a cycle fails (port busy, timeout), the previous files are not modified.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully (or daemon mode was terminated by signal).
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (device busy, timeout, checksum mismatch, upload
failure, etc.).
A message is printed to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/ttyACM0
Default serial port (USB CDC ACM, used by the ESP32 USB-JTAG/Serial interface).
.TP
.I ~/.power4ctl_history
REPL command history file, read at startup and written on exit.
Up to 200 entries are retained.
.TP
.I /run/power4/
Default daemon output directory.
.SH LOCKING
.B power4ctl
calls
.BR flock (2)
with
.B LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB
and sets
.B TIOCEXCL
on the port immediately after opening it.
In single-shot mode, if another process already holds the port the tool
exits immediately with an error rather than waiting.
In daemon mode, the lock attempt is retried every 500\ ms for up to
.I lock\-seconds
seconds (default\ 5) before the cycle is skipped.
These locks apply only to serial connections.
.SH NOTES
The port is configured in raw 8N1 mode with the requested baud rate and
no hardware flow control.
For USB CDC ACM devices the baud rate setting is advisory and the
device may ignore it.
.PP
The prompt is elicited by sending a carriage return and waiting for
.BR "power4> " .
Up to three attempts are made, sharing the timeout evenly.
.PP
SHA-1 verification uses a self-contained FIPS\~180-4 implementation with
no external library dependency.
.PP
TCP authentication uses HMAC-SHA256.
It authenticates access to the console but does not encrypt the subsequent
commands or responses.
